---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
---

<Layout title="Sideboard Analysis - Replenish Guide">
    <PageHeader
        title="Sideboard Analysis"
        description="An analytical card-by-card breakdown of the Premodern Replenish sideboard architecture, evaluating Tier 1 tournament staples, high-leverage metagame bullets, and modular utility slots."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/opalescence.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Opalescence"
        imageCaption="Opalescence by John Avon"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Metagame Data Methodology" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 0;"
        >
            All sideboard tier classifications and statistical inclusion
            percentages throughout this guide are derived from an exhaustive,
            rigorous analysis of <strong
                >180 winning Premodern tournament decklists</strong
            > (aggregated via MTGDecks). To ensure tactical accuracy for contemporary
            competitive play, our database explicitly filters for events conducted
            <strong>strictly post-ban</strong> (following the departure of <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a
            >). Removing historical "Tide-era" noise reveals true modern
            tournament consensus—highlighting the universal necessity of
            proactive mana protection and transformational post-board win
            conditions.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Tier 1: Core Tournament Staples" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 2rem;"
        >
            The foundational reactive and transformative pillars of competitive
            Premodern Replenish sideboards. Based on real post-ban tournament
            data, these slots address universal format imbalances and belong in
            virtually every optimized 75.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Exalted Angel"
            image="creatures/exalted_angel.jpg"
            badge="2–3 Side · 95.6% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                The quintessential post-sideboard hammer, introduced across
                virtually every non-combo matchup. Against red aggression, even
                deploying it as a face-down morph blocker that absorbs a burn
                spell preserves critical life. Against control, it demands
                physical removal that opponents frequently trimmed after Game 1.
                Against mana-denial builds (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Terravore">Terrageddon</a
                >), establishing an early Angel clock consistently deters
                opponents from executing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Armageddon">Armageddon</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>The Transformational Advantage:</strong> Because opponents
                predictably side out spot removal in favor of enchantment sweepers
                and graveyard extraction (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >), an early morph transition frequently dominates UW Control,
                Rock, and Ponza while they clutch inert hate pieces.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Meddling Mage"
            image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp"
            badge="3–4 Side · 92.2% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                A defining powerhouse of the post-ban sideboard metagame. With
                over 92% of competitive sideboards fielding an average of 3 to 4
                copies (and another 10% running copies maindeck), <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > represents an absolute tournament consensus for Games 2 and 3. Deploying
                Mage post-board operates on a devastating dual axis: it proactively
                locks out critical enemy bottleneck spells while contributing to a
                fast, transformational clock alongside Exalted Angel when opponents
                trim their spot removal.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Surgical Naming Protocol:</strong> Against graveyard hate,
                naming <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                > or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a
                > clears the runway for an unhindered Replenish loop. Against control,
                shutting down <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Counterspell">Counterspell</a
                > or enchantment sweepers guarantees combo resolves on schedule. For
                exhaustive matchup-by-match naming conventions, consult our <a
                    href="/matchups"
                    style="color: var(--w-color-info); text-decoration: underline; font-weight: bold;"
                    >Matchup Matrix</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tsabo's Web"
            image="artifacts/tsabos_web.jpg"
            badge="1–3 Side · 77.8% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                While pilots fielding maindeck copies utilize <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                > as broad proactive disruption, over 77% of tournament players rely
                on it in the sideboard specifically to counter Games 2 and 3 resource
                attrition. In post-board games against aggressive shells (Goblins,
                Rock, Ponza), opponents predictably double down on mana-denial loops
                and land taxation to choke your four-mana setup turns before you can
                answer their graveyard hate. Siding in Web acts as dedicated structural
                mana armor, completely locking down enemy disruption and ensuring
                your mana develops on schedule while preserving card velocity through
                its cantrip draw.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Defense Grid"
            image="artifacts/defense_grid.jpg"
            badge="0–3 Side · 52.8% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The premier resource equalizer against permission-heavy
                structures. By forcing opponents to pay an additional three mana
                to play spells during your turn, it neutralizes countermagic and
                allows your four-mana combo setups to deploy uninhibited.
                Furthermore, it operates seamlessly alongside cycling <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                > on opposing end steps.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Hydroblast / Blue Elemental Blast"
            image="instants/hydroblast.jpg"
            badge="2–4 Side · 76.1% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The cleanest one-mana answer to rapid red clocks and disruptive
                sideboard tech. Whether countering an inbound <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Pyroblast"
                    >Pyroblast / Red Elemental Blast</a
                > targeting your blue combo engines, removing a turn-one <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Goblin Lackey">Goblin Lackey</a
                > on sight before it cheats lethal boards into play, or answering
                game-breaking enchantment hate like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Anarchy">Anarchy</a
                >, Hydroblast represents strictly indispensable defensive
                velocity.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Seal of Cleansing / Disenchant"
            image="enchantments/seal_of_cleansing.webp"
            badge="1–3 Side · 50.0% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>The Seal/Disenchant Split:</strong> In modern tournament builds,
                <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > dominates post-board artifact and enchantment interaction (fielded
                in 50% of sideboards and 97.8% of maindecks) due to its seamless recursion
                via <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                > and animation into a 2/2 threat under <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                >. However, with the dominance of <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                > across contemporary tournaments, relying exclusively on Seal post-board
                presents vulnerability to two-mana static taxation. Maintaining 1
                or 2 copies of <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > (3.9% inclusion) provides immune, instant-speed interaction that
                cleanly bypasses Aura's cost penalty.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Abeyance"
            image="instants/abeyance.jpg"
            badge="1–2 Side · 42.2% SB / 72.8% Main"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                For the 42.2% of competitive pilots fielding <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                > in the sideboard, its Games 2 and 3 objective expands far beyond
                simple combo protection. When facing opponents who aggressively transition
                into instantaneous graveyard hate after Game 1 (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
                >, or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Scour"
                    >Scour</a
                >), bringing in supplementary copies of Abeyance allows you to
                pivot into an upkeep disruption plan. By casting it during an
                opponent's turn, you simultaneously paralyze their activated
                hate pieces and freeze their reactive spell mana—buying critical
                tempo for transformational threats like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                > to dominate the battlefield.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Wrath of God"
            image="sorceries/wrath_of_god.webp"
            badge="20.0% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The foundational sweeping reset against swarming tribal
                formations and accelerated midrange boards. Surging to 20%
                post-ban inclusion as an essential defensive equalizer, in
                regional environments saturated with Elves, Goblins, or Green
                White aggro shells, supplementing spot removal with board
                sweepers buys the necessary breathing room to assemble your
                enchantment lock.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Tier 2: High-Leverage Metagame Bullets" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 2rem;"
        >
            Targeted weapons and defensive architectures calibrated to
            neutralize specific archetype saturations and aggressive post-board
            hate suites. <strong>Why aren't these universally adopted?</strong> Unlike
            Tier 1 pillars that solve broad structural problems across diverse matchups,
            Tier 2 cards are narrow, reactionary adjustments designed specifically
            for targeted regional metagames (such as environments heavily saturated
            with aggressive Red or mana-denial loops). Because your 15 sideboard slots
            are finite, dedicating excessive room to narrow technical bullets dilutes
            your proactive transformational plans.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Anti-Red Defense: Sphere of Law vs. Warmth vs. CoP: Red"
            image="enchantments/sphere_of_law.jpg"
            badge="Theory & Comparison · 20.0% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Selecting the correct red defense suite requires rigorous
                competitive analysis of your mana tolerances and anticipated
                damage profiles:
            </p>
            <ul style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Sphere of Law (Recommended):</strong> Simply the superior
                    four-mana solution against both Goblins and Sligh, leading the
                    post-ban format at 20% inclusion. Subtracting two points of damage
                    passively from <em>every</em> source creates an unbreachable defensive
                    wall without consuming ongoing mana. The acknowledged tradeoff
                    is that aggressive red pilots will heavily side in <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Anarchy">Anarchy</a
                    > as counter-hate, requiring prompt countermagic readiness.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Warmth:</strong> Exceptional against spell-slinging Sligh
                    builds where gaining two life per red spell nullifies burn curves.
                    However, it is structurally flawed against Goblins—recurring combat
                    damage from aggressive creatures rapidly overwhelms the temporary
                    lifegain buffer, rendering Warmth severely one-dimensional.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <strong>Circle of Protection: Red:</strong> While universally
                    protective, CoP is fatally <em>"mana hungry."</em> In our proactive
                    combo-control architecture, where mana utilization is extremely
                    tight for <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                    > cycling and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                    > velocity, we lack the luxury of remaining untapped like a passive
                    draw-go deck. Continually taxing your own generic mana directly
                    stifles combo assembly, rendering CoP sub-optimal here.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Chill"
            image="enchantments/chill.jpg"
            badge="2–3 Side · 10.0% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                While <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Chill"
                    >Chill</a
                > is an exceptional engine against traditional Sligh and Burn—doubling
                the casting cost of red spells to paralyze burn velocity—it presents
                a critical structural vulnerability against Goblins.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                Against Goblins, aggressive pilots frequently resolve a turn-one <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Goblin Lackey">Goblin Lackey</a
                > before Chill can ever touch the stack. A connecting Lackey trigger
                bypasses spell-casting entirely, dumping lethal siege threats straight
                onto the battlefield and rendering your two-mana tax completely superfluous.
                Because it exists as an enchantment, discarded copies naturally recur
                via <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                >, but it should never be solely relied upon as anti-Goblins
                tech without supplemental one-mana spot removal.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Orim's Chant"
            image="instants/orims_chant.webp"
            badge="1–2 Side · 13.3% Inclusion"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Historically, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Orim's Chant">Orim's Chant</a
                > was a premier protection staple during the Parallax Tide era. When
                rushing to resolve a turn-three or turn-four Tide to banish five enemy
                lands in a devastating 5-for-1 blowout, paying just one mana (even
                at the strict cost of card disadvantage) was an acceptable trade-off
                against reactive blue decks.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Why Abeyance Eclipsed Chant Post-Ban:</strong> As contemporary
                tournament theory established, the banning of Parallax Tide fundamentally
                altered control counterplay; modern Replenish no longer operates under
                rushed turn-four deadlines against blue decks that present zero clock,
                making a one-mana discount immaterial. Furthermore, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                > completely eclipses Chant across three tactical axes: it draws a
                card upon resolution (zero card disadvantage), it actively shuts down
                lethal activated abilities (freezing an on-board <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                > or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
                > before comboing), and it generates unmatched defensive tempo against
                mirror and Enchantress loops. Today, Chant remains a secondary option
                only when hyper-efficient upkeep delay against aggressive combo is
                strictly prioritized.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Annul"
            image="instants/annul.jpg"
            badge="1–2 Side · Instant Answer"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                An exceptionally versatile one-mana permission bullet that
                solves problem permanents directly on the stack—neutralizing
                static taxers before they constrain our mana or when waiting to
                crack an on-board <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > carries lethal timing risks.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Exhaustive Premodern Target Matrix:</strong> Maintaining Annul
                in your 15 yields super-efficient 1-for-1 trades against format-defining
                gamebreakers: <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sphere of Resistance"
                    >Sphere of Resistance</a
                >, opposing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                    >Parallax Wave</a
                > (crucial in mirror battles), <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Standstill">Standstill</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Aluren"
                    >Aluren</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Dream Halls"
                    >Dream Halls</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Mox Diamond"
                    >Mox Diamond</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Cursed Totem"
                    >Cursed Totem</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sterling Grove">Sterling Grove</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Zombie Infestation">Zombie Infestation</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Devourer">Phyrexian Devourer</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Altar of Dementia">Altar of Dementia</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Survival of the Fittest"
                    >Survival of the Fittest</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Alter Reality"
            image="instants/alter_reality.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side · Regional Tech"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                Highly precise regional tech deployed specifically to counter <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                > from black disruptive piles. By modulating the word "White" to "Black"
                on their resolved Gloom, the tax reverses entirely onto the opponent's
                own spell curve. Its embedded Flashback cost ensures functional utility
                even under discarded constraints.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Tier 3: Niche & Modular Technology" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 2rem;"
        >
            Specialized tactical tools, surgical interactions, and fringe
            candidate slots seeing under 10% adoption across post-ban
            tournaments. <strong>Why do these see so little usage?</strong> Most Tier
            3 entries fall into two distinct structural categories: either they are
            hyper-specialized bullets targeted at rare fringe matchups (such as Reanimator
            or pure graveyard loops), or they represent historical pre-ban philosophies
            whose functionality has been cleanly outclassed by modular 4-of transformational
            staples like Meddling Mage and Exalted Angel. While theoretically viable,
            fielding these slots requires explicit, high-confidence reads on your
            local tournament environment.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tormod's Crypt"
            image="artifacts/tormods_crypt.jpg"
            badge="0–3 Side · Surgical Tech"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The zero-mana benchmark for graveyard interaction. Critical
                against accelerated Reanimator loops, explosive Phyrexian
                Devourer combo structures, and tactical Replenish mirrors. In
                mirror matches, mastery requires clinical timing: preserve your
                Crypt untapped on the table and sacrifice it strictly in
                response to an opponent's terminal <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > resolution or an <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                > activation on the stack, obliterating their setup while leaving
                your own graveyard architecture intact.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Nevinyrral's Disk"
            image="artifacts/nevinyrrals_disk.jpg"
            badge="0–2 Side · Universal Reset"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The premier universal reset button against intricate permanent
                locks, heavy midrange boards, and resolved anti-combo artifacts.
                In protracted attrition battles (e.g., The Rock, UW Control),
                detonating an active Disk not only wipes opposing creatures and
                hate pieces simultaneously, but milimetrically drops your own
                fielded enchantments directly into your graveyard—preparing a
                devastating follow-up <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > onto a completely clear board.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Light of Day"
            image="enchantments/light_of_day.jpg"
            badge="1–2 Side · Silver Bullet"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The ultimate structural lockdown against black creature-driven
                archetypes. Against Suicide Black, Psychatog, or aggressive The
                Rock formations, deploying a resolved <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Light of Day">Light of Day</a
                > establishes an absolute prohibition: black creatures are permanently
                forbidden from attacking or blocking. Furthermore, its four-mana enchantment
                card type ensures seamless recursion with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > if stripped early by opposing discard suites.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Seal of Removal (Proactive Creature Bounce)"
            image="enchantments/seal_of_removal.jpg"
            badge="Modular Tech"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                A low-cost enchantment-based bounce bullet that excels against
                explosive early deployments like Turn 1 <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Goblin Lackey">Goblin Lackey</a
                > or high-pressure threats possessing protection from white. Deploying
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Removal"
                    >Seal of Removal</a
                > buys vital setup turns for combo assembly while contributing recurring
                permanent velocity to your graveyard pool for subsequent <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > loops.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Strategic Critique & Tradeoff:</strong> Unlike terminal exile
                via spot removal (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                >), relying on interactive bounce offers temporary defensive
                respite rather than a permanent solution. Furthermore,
                dedicating sideboard slots to early bounce can degrade in
                utility during slower attrition matches where raw card advantage
                is paramount.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Spiritual Focus"
            image="enchantments/spiritual_focus.jpg"
            badge="Anti-Discard Tech"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                A specialized defensive enchantment that harshly penalizes
                aggressive black discard architectures by drawing two cards
                whenever an opponent forces a hand drop via <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Hymn to Tourach">Hymn to Tourach</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gerrard's Verdict">Gerrard's Verdict</a
                >, or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Duress"
                    >Duress</a
                >. While select veteran pilots value its ability to out-grind
                heavy attrition loops, it remains inherently slow and
                passive—frequently underperforming in fast-paced tempo
                environments where initiative is paramount.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Stifle"
            image="instants/stifle.jpg"
            badge="Surgical Tech"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                Historically a mandatory staple during competitive eras
                dominated by High Tide and Reset combo shells, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Stifle">Stifle</a
                > exists today as a foundational relic in traditional Premodern Replenish
